#2.1
Area Ratio (replaces Circularity): compare the area implied by the horizontal and vertical radii against the area implied by the mean radius.
  s1 = area of horizontal radius / area of mean radius
  s2 = area of vertical radius   / area of mean radius
Accept if:  0.85 < s1 < 1.15  AND  0.85 < s2 < 1.15
Logic: used so that the segmentation calculations do not go wrong.
Why area and not perimeter: the conventional circularity 4*pi*A/P^2 squares the perimeter, so a ragged FOV boundary inflates P and collapses the score. Measured on APTOS: 53.5% of all images fall outside 0.85-1.15, including 53.0% of images whose circle-fit residual (<0.02) says the FOV is intact. Perimeter-based circularity is therefore not usable as a gate.
Why area and not radius: area scales as r^2, so the same 0.85-1.15 bounds are roughly twice as strict as they would be on radius directly (a radius ratio of 0.92-1.07 maps to an area ratio of 0.85-1.15). This is a deliberate tightening.
Method to reduce compute: derive the horizontal and vertical radii from the FOV mask extents; no boundary tracing required.
Fails: Failed Image Quality Requirements (avoiding false-negative case from occurring)

#2.2
Black-region of image: band depends on ASPECT RATIO (width/height); RGB: (0,0,0)
  aspect 1.00  ->  15 - 25%
  aspect 1.33  ->  15 - 45%
  aspect 1.39  ->  15 - 47.5%
  aspect 1.51  ->  15 - 50%
  (use the nearest specified aspect band)
Logic: ratio of circle circumscribed by square is 78.5%, so a square frame with an inscribed retina is ~21.5% black. A non-square frame cannot reach that figure: an inscribed circle covers pi/(4*a) of a frame of aspect a, so the black fraction rises with aspect ratio. A single flat band therefore rejects correctly-framed wide images.
Geometric expectation per aspect (black %): 1.00 -> 21.5%, 1.33 -> 40.9%, 1.39 -> 43.5%, 1.51 -> 48.0%. All four sit inside the bands above.
Measured on APTOS: the banded gate rejects 22.4% of images (a flat 15-35% band rejected 25.1%). Residual failures are concentrated in aspect 1.33 (median black 52.7%, above the 45% ceiling) and aspect 1.32 (median 17.5%, far below geometry). Crop style varies within the same aspect ratio, so aspect alone does not fully determine the black fraction - revisit if these groups matter.
Method to reduce compute: pick random 10% of horizontal pixel arrays and check if 80% of the sets have black-region ratio inside the band for that aspect.
Fails: Failed Image Quality Requirements (avoiding false-negative case from occurring)

#3
Region Min is RETAINED: it is the only metric in this cascade that detects PARTIAL blur. Global metrics average a locally-blurred region away, so an image that is sharp everywhere except the macula passes every other test and is still clinically useless. Measured partial-blur sensitivity is ~4,100x. The deployment scenario is portable cameras operated by technicians with short training, not medical-grade capture, so partial defocus and local motion blur must be assumed.
SpecSlope can cleanly identify blood vessels (so crucial)
TenengradVar is a confirmation for the process Variance of Laplacian (NORMED)

